.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH postar 1  "postar " 
.SH NAME
postar \- The main struct describing the CLI args
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpostar\fR [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-rules\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-server\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-polling\-delay\fR] [\fB\-\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The main struct describing the CLI args
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to the TOML config file.

This specifies things like default flags and all the connection details.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rules\fR \fI<RULES>\fR
Path to the PTAR rules file.

This specifies how the emails should be filtered and which actions should be executed upon rule match.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fI<LOG>\fR [default: info]
The logging level
.br

.br
[\fIpossible values: \fRoff, error, warn, info, debug, trace]
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-server\fR \fI<SERVER>\fR
The server that postar connects to.

It can be either specified in the config file by settings the default option to true or by passing in this flag.
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<DB>\fR
Path to the persistent database. Ordinary users should not change this option
.TP
\fB\-\-polling\-delay\fR \fI<POLLING_DELAY>\fR
The polling delay when using the polling method for inboxes.

This is relevant when the IDLE capability for IMAP inboxes is not available so the program must poll. This can be either specified as a flag or in the config file.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Check whether the configuration is valid
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Perform a dry run on the most recent 10 messages
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
postar\-completions(1)
Outputs shell completions to stdout
.TP
postar\-init(1)
Intializes the configuration files
